WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:04.690
Speaker 1: We have seen a lot of things get
kind of wacky and backwards in the aftermath

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:06.980
of Joe Biden pulling troops from Afghanistan.

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:12.080
And, of course, that created a vacuum in which
the Taliban has retaken control of the country.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:16.570
Now, I'm not going to redo yesterday's segment
where I outlined my position on this in full.

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:19.380
But in short, I agree with Joe Biden's decision.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:20.840
I said this yesterday.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:25.900
I think there would have been chaos whenever
we ultimately left Afghanistan.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:29.060
But the Afghanistan incursion has been a failure.

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:32.320
More U.S. involvement for more time wouldn't
help anything.

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:36.810
The longer we stay, the more money is spent
to ultimately achieve nothing.

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:37.960
Our involvement is a negative.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:39.710
It's not a positive.

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:44.480
And one of the things that we're seeing is
all sorts of people on the left, on the right,

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:48.079
for different reasons now say we have to go
back into Afghanistan.

00:00:48.079 --> 00:00:49.310
That's one thing we're seeing.

00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:51.790
There's all of a sudden people saying, no,
we got to go back.

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:53.110
It was a mistake to leave.

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:56.670
We've got to do more nation building and peacekeeping
and world policing and all this stuff.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:57.860
Let's go back in.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:00:59.219
That's weird.

00:00:59.219 --> 00:01:03.360
But another of the strange things we're seeing,
and I'm not even saying I know what to make

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:09.329
of it, but another thing we're seeing is some
really bizarre, kind of like revisionist histories

00:01:09.329 --> 00:01:12.920
and apologizing for the Taliban, which is
now taking control.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:19.850
Now, I will be the first to admit the Taliban
is more complicated than just like one explanation,

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:21.170
like Sharia law.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:27.389
OK, well, I mean, Sharia law can mean different
things in different countries, but there are

00:01:27.389 --> 00:01:33.170
now some people framing the Taliban as sort
of like maybe kind of good.

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:35.689
And I'm finding it really weird.

00:01:35.689 --> 00:01:41.540
And today we're going to look at a clip of
British Chief of Defense Staff General Sir

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:43.169
Nick Carter.

00:01:43.169 --> 00:01:44.169
That's actually his title.

00:01:44.169 --> 00:01:50.569
I'm not like doing the sir routine, General
Sir Nick Carter, who was on Sky News talking

00:01:50.569 --> 00:01:53.630
about kind of like the Taliban is not that
bad.

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:57.579
And so let's just get into it, check out the
words he uses and how he describes the Taliban.

00:01:57.579 --> 00:02:02.999
Now, Carter was in Afghanistan in 2004, commanding
British troops there had additional deployments

00:02:02.999 --> 00:02:04.130
to Afghanistan.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:10.770
And remember, the Taliban is a radical group
considered a terrorist group by multiple governments,

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:12.570
including Canada, Turkey and others.

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:16.870
The U.S. has never recognized the Taliban
government, although I don't believe the U.S.

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:21.410
considers the Taliban a terrorist group Trump
was just negotiating with the months ago.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:22.410
So here we go.

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:23.760
Let's take a look and check this out.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:29.000
Speaker 2: And how do you feel about collaborating
with the enemy when they have carried out

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.410
such atrocities against U.S. military personnel
base?

00:02:32.410 --> 00:02:36.150
Speaker 3: I think you have to be very careful
using the word enemy.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:40.291
I think people need to understand who the
Taliban actually are and of course, what they

00:02:40.291 --> 00:02:45.050
are, a disparate collection of tribes people,
as President Karzai put it to me only yesterday,

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:46.890
the country boys.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:50.680
And the plain fact is that they happen to
live by a code of honor.

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:57.540
Speaker 1: So these are these are tribes people,
their country boys, and they live by a code

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:01.650
of honor, almost implying like that's a good
thing on its face.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:03.900
Now, code of honor, code of honor can be anything.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:07.310
You can have a code of honor that is horrifying.

00:03:07.310 --> 00:03:10.280
And there are many examples of that.

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:13.060
But he continues to sort of say this is not
good.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:16.880
This is maybe not so bad of a group
Speaker 3: to stand it, which has been the

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:17.910
standard for many, many years.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:19.830
It's called Pashtunwali.

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:21.770
It has honor at the heart of what they do.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:26.870
They are bound together by a common purpose,
which is they don't like corrupt governance,

00:03:26.870 --> 00:03:31.130
they don't like governance that is self-serving
and they want an Afghanistan that is inclusive

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:32.130
for all.

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:33.130
Speaker 1: Wow.

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:34.740
I mean, that that's sounding pretty good,
right?

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:36.770
I mean, it's all about honor.

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:38.490
These are country boys.

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:40.180
They're very much against corruption.

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:41.430
Will corruptions bad?

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:44.880
So being against corruption must be good and
they're for inclusion.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.590
I mean, that's all pretty good stuff right
now.

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:53.890
While in power in Afghanistan, when you look
at the history, the Taliban became notorious

00:03:53.890 --> 00:04:00.250
for their misogyny and their violence against
women, period, forcing full burqa wearing

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:07.460
at all at all times, systematic gender segregation,
women not allowed to work, women not allowed

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:12.170
to be educated past age eight, only allowed
to go to a male doctor if they have a male

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:13.860
chaperon to take them.

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:19.400
Public floggings and executions for women
for violating Taliban law, occasionally forcing

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:21.019
into child marriage.

00:04:21.019 --> 00:04:22.870
And on and on and on.

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:26.900
So maybe he's saying it would be different
now or something.

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:29.139
But but we don't actually have reason to think
that that's the case.

00:04:29.139 --> 00:04:30.190
So let's continue here.

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:33.699
Speaker 3: So I think rather than talking
about women, but except women.

00:04:33.699 --> 00:04:35.749
Well, again, I think we have to wait and see.

00:04:35.749 --> 00:04:37.389
I mean, I don't know what they mean.

00:04:37.389 --> 00:04:39.800
Speaker 2: I'll support that the way that
they treat women, actually.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:42.830
Speaker 3: Well, I think you have to listen
to what they're saying at the moment.

00:04:42.830 --> 00:04:46.039
And I think you have to listen to the facts
on the ground saying they are justified

00:04:46.039 --> 00:04:51.610
Speaker 1: by what they're saying is using
this coded language about the involvement

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:52.610
of women in.

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:59.509
Society as dictated by religious law, right,
and so you have to be you have to understand

00:04:59.509 --> 00:05:02.130
that that's an extraordinarily cynical way
to talk about inclusion.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:03.130
Speaker 3: Oh, yeah.

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:06.729
And I'm not saying that's anything that you
and I would approve of, particularly.

00:05:06.729 --> 00:05:07.729
I'm just.

00:05:07.729 --> 00:05:08.729
But I do find that.

00:05:08.729 --> 00:05:09.729
Absolutely.

00:05:09.729 --> 00:05:10.729
But I do.

00:05:10.729 --> 00:05:11.729
Speaker 1: And I'll go back just a second,
because Sharia law is mentioned here, acts

00:05:11.729 --> 00:05:13.020
on the
Speaker 3: ground saying they are justified.

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:17.099
So, yeah, and I'm not saying that's anything
that you and I would approve of particularly.

00:05:17.099 --> 00:05:18.409
I'm just not.

00:05:18.409 --> 00:05:19.409
Absolutely.

00:05:19.409 --> 00:05:20.590
But I do think that they have changed.

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:25.280
I think they recognize that over the course
of the last 20 years, Afghanistan has evolved.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:28.509
They recognize the fundamental role that women
have played in that evolution.

00:05:28.509 --> 00:05:33.919
And, yes, they at the moment will undoubtedly
say that they want to respect women's rights

00:05:33.919 --> 00:05:36.330
under Islamic law, and that will be a Sharia
law.

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:40.110
But that doesn't necessarily mean that they
won't allow them to be involved in government

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:44.229
and in education and in medicine and those
things that they need them to be involved

00:05:44.229 --> 00:05:45.229
in.

00:05:45.229 --> 00:05:49.379
Speaker 1: Now, I don't want to just slam
this guy like there is.

00:05:49.379 --> 00:05:53.740
There's an aspect of this that I think, you
know, we I am not for Sharia law.

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:59.530
I'm not for any religious law being the government
law, civil law, OK?

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:02.620
No matter what it is, I don't want the Christian
version of Sharia that some of these right

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:03.740
wingers want in the US.

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:07.889
I don't mean I'm against Sharia law, but if
we're going to have a more nuanced understanding

00:06:07.889 --> 00:06:10.919
of this, Sharia can mean different things
in different places.

00:06:10.919 --> 00:06:15.400
And we have to understand that, like, for
example, there's full Sharia criminal law,

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:16.400
which is outrageous.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:19.620
I mean, just completely outrageous.

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:27.400
There's also Sharia law, like in Egypt, where
the penal and civil codes are basically based

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:33.139
on 19th century French law, as in as is the
case in many parts of the world.

00:06:33.139 --> 00:06:41.620
But but there is Sharia law in the country
in some more general religious going to mix

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:42.620
with the government.

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:46.990
But the penal code, the civil law is still
is still based on 19th century French law.

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:53.720
So the point here is it's true that Sharia
law is not one thing, but there's no reason

00:06:53.720 --> 00:07:00.960
to think based on the history of the Taliban,
that they would go in the direction of Egypt.

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:07.569
Historically, the Taliban has gone more in
the direction of full Sharia criminal law.

00:07:07.569 --> 00:07:10.090
Let's let's look just the last few seconds
of this clip.

00:07:10.090 --> 00:07:11.869
Speaker 3: So I think we have to be patient.

00:07:11.869 --> 00:07:16.639
We have to give them the space to show how
they are going to step up to the plate and

00:07:16.639 --> 00:07:20.310
whether or not we can work with them will
very much depend upon how they treat all Afghans.

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:25.370
Speaker 1: OK, so it's sort of just like a
weird soft apologetics for the Taliban.

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:26.849
And this is just one example.

00:07:26.849 --> 00:07:31.969
And to be clear, I'm not saying the Taliban's
bad, so we got to send troops and no, I'm

00:07:31.969 --> 00:07:35.169
glad the troops are coming out, even though
some now went back in.

00:07:35.169 --> 00:07:37.249
We talked about that yesterday.

00:07:37.249 --> 00:07:41.539
But some of the weird apologetics that we're
seeing for the Taliban, absent any reason

00:07:41.539 --> 00:07:43.809
to think that the Taliban has really changed.

00:07:43.809 --> 00:07:45.099
I just find bizarre.

00:07:45.099 --> 00:07:46.400
So I want to hear from you about this.

